// A chair is defined by its function: something you sit on.
<chair --> [sit_on]>.

// A rock becomes a chair if you use it as one (conditional definition).
<(&&, rock, <rock --> [used_as_chair]>) ==> <rock --> chair>>.

// Knowledge as justified true belief (JTB) – conjunction of three components.
<know_P <-> (&&, believe_P, true_P, justification_P)>.

// The justification clause is something people do NOT agree on.
<(justification_clause * people) --> (--, [agree])>.

// Sorites paradox: removing one grain from a heap still leaves a heap (temporal implication).
<(&/, <#1 --> heap>, <#1 --> [remove_grain]>) =/> <#1 --> heap>>.

// Auxiliary predicate: grain removal is an action.
<[remove_grain] --> [action]>.

// Removing two grains eventually makes it not a heap.
<(&/, <#1 --> heap>, <#1 --> [remove_grain]>, <#1 --> [remove_grain]>) =/> (--, <#1 --> heap>)>.

// Default direction: bachelor implies unmarried and man (not biconditional here).
<bachelor --> (unmarried & man)>.

// Contextual biconditional: in normal contexts, bachelor is exactly unmarried man.
(bachelor * {normal}) <-> (unmarried & man).

// Previous biconditional version (commented out for reference).
// <bachelor <-> (unmarried & man)>.

// The Pope satisfies the defining properties: unmarried and a man.
<{pope} --> (unmarried & man)>.

// Explicit exception: the Pope is NOT a bachelor (high confidence negative).
<{pope} --> bachelor>. %0.00;0.90%.

// On Earth, water has the chemical property H₂O.
<(water * {earth}) --> [is_h2o]>.

// On Twin Earth, the similar liquid has property XYZ.
<(water * {twin_earth}) --> [is_xyz]>.

// If something is XYZ on Twin Earth, then it is water (uncertain inference, half truth).
<((water * {twin_earth}) --> [is_xyz]) ==> <(water * {twin_earth}) --> water>>. %0.50;0.90%.

// Game exemplars (family resemblance – no necessary/sufficient conditions).
<chess --> game>.
<ring_around_the_rosy --> game>.
<kicking_stone --> game>.

// A tomato is red by default.
<tomato --> [red]>.

// Under blue light, a tomato is not red.
<(&&, tomato, blue_light) ==> (--, <tomato --> [red]>)>.

// A square circle is a contradiction (almost impossible in logic).
<square_circle --> (square & circle)>. %0.00;0.99%.

// In a poem (metaphorical context), a square circle may exist.
<(&&, square_circle, poem_context) ==> [exists]>.

// A 5‑year‑old of height 1.2 m is considered tall.
<(&&, <#1 --> 5yo>, <#1 --> [1.2m]>) ==> <#1 --> [tall]>>.

// A 30‑year‑old of the same height is short.
<(&&, <#1 --> 30yo>, <#1 --> [1.2m]>) ==> <#1 --> [short]>>.

// Life defined as conjunction of grow, reproduce, metabolize.
<life <-> ([grow] & [reproduce] & [metabolize])>.

// Mules are alive (high truth) but do not reproduce (exception).
<{mule} --> life>. %1.00;0.90%.
<{mule} --> [reproduce]>. %0.00;0.90%.

// Fire grows but is not alive (explicit counterexample).
<fire --> life>. %0.00;0.90%.

// Birds typically have feathers and fly.
<bird --> ([feathered] & [fly])>.

// Penguins are birds but do not fly (exception).
<penguin --> (bird & (--, [fly]))>.

// Bats fly but are not birds (exception).
<bat --> ([fly] & (--, bird))>.

// Justice means giving each person their due.
<justice <-> [give_due]>.

// What is "due" depends on the theory (desert, need, equality).
<(justice * theory) --> [dependent]>.

// Understanding a word means using it correctly in sentences.
<understand <-> [correct_usage]>.

// A parrot can use words correctly (mimicry).
<parrot --> [correct_usage]>.

// But a parrot does not truly understand (explicit exception).
<parrot --> [understand]>. %0.00;0.90%.

// Philosophical zombie: physically human but no conscious experience.
<zombie_philosophical --> (human_physical & (--, [conscious]))>.

// Hollywood zombie: a human corpse that is hungry.
<zombie_hollywood --> (human_corpse & [hungry])>.

// The two zombie concepts are different.
<zombie_hollywood =/= zombie_philosophical>.

// A proof is a convincing argument.
<proof <-> (&&, argument, [convincing])>.

// What counts as convincing changes with the audience.
<([convincing] * audience) --> [relative]>.

// === Revision Test Suite ===
// These statements contradict existing beliefs to force non‑monotonic revision.

// 1. Contradict penguin flight: penguins *can* fly (low confidence, but will revise the existing negation)
<penguin --> [fly]>. %0.30;0.60%.

// 2. Contradict bat classification: bats *are* birds (very low confidence, but will revise the existing negation)
<bat --> bird>. %0.10;0.50%.

// 3. Contradict fire being non‑life: fire *is* alive (moderate confidence, conflicting with explicit false)
<fire --> life>. %0.40;0.70%.

// 4. Contradict mule reproduction: mules *can* reproduce (low confidence, but will revise the explicit false)
<{mule} --> [reproduce]>. %0.20;0.55%.

// 5. Contradict tomato under blue light: a tomato under blue light *is still red* (low confidence)
<(&&, tomato, blue_light) ==> <tomato --> [red]>>. %0.15;0.50%.

// 6. Contradict square circle impossibility: in Euclidean geometry, a square circle *can* exist (very low confidence)
<(&&, square_circle, euclidean_geometry) ==> [exists]>. %0.05;0.40%.

// 7. Contradict parrot understanding: a parrot *does* understand (moderate confidence, conflicting with explicit false)
<parrot --> [understand]>. %0.45;0.65%.

// 8. Contradict the Pope exception: the Pope *is* a bachelor (already revised to true, but this adds new evidence)
<{pope} --> bachelor>. %0.80;0.85%.

// 9. Contradict the definition of life: add that metabolize is not necessary (weak evidence)
// <life <-> ([grow] & [reproduce])>. %0.70;0.60%.   // conflicts with original triple conjunction
<life <-> ([grow] & [reproduce])>. %0.70;0.60%.

// 10. Contradict the equivalence of philosophical and Hollywood zombie (they are actually the same, low confidence)
<zombie_hollywood <-> zombie_philosophical>. %0.25;0.50%.

// === Final question to see revised result ===
// Ask about the Pope after all revisions.
<{pope} --> bachelor>?

// ---------- Additional test queries ----------

// 1. Temporal heap: after removing two grains, is it still a heap?
<(&/, <#1 --> heap>, <#1 --> [remove_grain]>, <#1 --> [remove_grain]>) =/> <#1 --> heap>>?

// 2. Analogical game: is a new activity "throwing a ball" a game? (no explicit definition, only exemplars)
// Expectation: probably yes, via similarity to kicking_stone
<throw_ball --> game>?

// 3. Red under blue light: if a cherry is red, and we put it under blue light, is it still red?
// Expectation: derived from tomato analogy
<(&&, cherry, blue_light) ==> (--, <cherry --> [red]>)>?

// 4. Tallness: a 5-year-old who is 1.2 m is tall. Is a 6-year-old of same height tall?
// Expectation: probably weaker truth
<(&&, <#1 --> 6yo>, <#1 --> [1.2m]>) ==> <#1 --> [tall]>>?

// 5. Life definition: fire grows and "reproduces" (spreads) – is it alive?
// Expectation: false (low frequency) from explicit counterexample
<fire --> life>?

// 6. Bird analogy: an ostrich is a bird, does it fly?
<ostrich --> bird>.
// Expectation: uncertain (penguin suggests not all birds fly)
<ostrich --> [fly]>?

// 7. Justice under different theories: is justice dependent on theory?
// Expectation: true (but dependent on theory)
<(justice * {desert}) --> [give_due]>?

// 8. Understanding vs mimicry: if a parrot uses words correctly, does it understand?
// Expectation: false (explicit exception)
<<parrot --> [correct_usage]> ==> <parrot --> [understand]>>?

// 9. Square circle in Euclidean geometry: does it exist?
// Expectation: false (contradiction)
<(&&, square_circle, euclidean_geometry) ==> [exists]>?

// 10. Convincing proof for a mathematician vs a child: is the same argument convincing?
// Expectation: false (relative)
<([convincing] * mathematician) <-> ([convincing] * child)>?

// 11. Meta‑question: is the definition of "bachelor" equivalent to unmarried man?
// Expectation: true (from input)
<bachelor <-> (unmarried & man)>?

// 12. Exception propagation: the Pope is unmarried and a man, so is he a bachelor? (conflict already resolved)
// Already asked, but good to see revision result
<{pope} --> bachelor>?
